# What this project is

We are updating the JRC fish provisioning ecosystem service accounts for the EU. A previous team
built the 2018 and 2023 assessments with a hand-run R script plus manual steps in Excel and QGIS.
We were hired to make that routine compact, reproducible and repeatable for future data vintages,
and to publish it as a Quarto site on GitHub Pages so the team can inspect it.

The source method is `reference/marine-methods.docx`, a JRC report. Chapter 4 covers fish
provisioning; Equations 1 to 7 and Boxes 1 and 2 are the ones the code implements. The original
script is `reference/old-processing-script.R`. `reference/emdb_processing.qmd` is a style reference
from another project, not part of this one.

The method in one paragraph: fish provisioning is valued with the resource rent approach. For each
fleet segment, net economic result equals income minus expenditure (Eq. 3). That single figure is
distributed across the sub-regions the fleet fished and then across the species it landed, weighted
by kilograms (Eq. 4 to 7). Fleets with a negative or zero net result cannot carry a valuation,
because an ecosystem service cannot have a negative flow, so their landings are valued from average
observed prices in three widening passes: same country, then same sub-region, then Europe-wide
(Box 2).

# Repository layout

- `_quarto.yml` sets `output-dir: docs`, `execute-dir: project`, and a `render:` list that excludes
  `data/` and `reference/`. That exclusion is load-bearing; see the QGIS trap below.
- `documentation/methods/marine-processing/index.qmd` is the main deliverable, the annotated
  processing routine.
- `documentation/methods/marine-methods/index.qmd` is a stub for the method write-up.
- `documentation/FS-landings-data/index.qmd` documents the STECF data conversion.
- `data/stecf/` holds the source workbook (gitignored), the conversion script, and two RDS outputs.
- `data/lookup/` holds three curated Excel lookups plus `hicp_eu27.csv`, the Eurostat HICP series
  used to deflate resource rent to 2010 euro.
- `data/gisdata/` holds `marine_regions.gpkg` and the script that built it.
- `data/outputs/results/` holds the published tables: `fish_provisioning.RDS` and a matching
  flat `fish_provisioning.xlsx`, plus `fish_provisioning_results.xlsx` (report-style SUTs, one
  year per sheet). `data/outputs/gis/fish_provisioning.gpkg` holds the map layers. These are
  versioned so they can be downloaded from the repo.

# Data

The source is `data/stecf/STECF_25 07_EU Fleet Economic and Transversal data_fleet segment
level.xlsx`, 285 MB, from the 2025 Fleet Economic data call. It is gitignored and must never be
committed. `data/stecf/stecf_to_rds.R` converts it; run `Rscript data/stecf/stecf_to_rds.R` from the
project root. It takes about 35 seconds and produces:

- `data/stecf/FS_landings_FAO_2008-2024.RDS`, 1,810,826 rows. Species by FAO sub-region, per fleet
  segment and year. Every observation appears twice, as `totwghtlandg` in kg and `totvallandg` in
  euro. Built from three sheets whose names understate their content; the tab labelled 2018-2023
  contains 2024.
- `data/stecf/FS_data_2008-2024.RDS`, 449,948 rows. Economic and transversal variables at fleet
  segment level, no species or sub-region dimension.

These are different tables that overlap in one variable. Kilograms exist only in the landings file;
income and expenditure exist only in `FS data`. The routine must read both. Their value-of-landings
series agree within three percent from 2009 to 2023.

Lookups in `data/lookup/`: `3.classification_vs_OFFICIAL.xlsx` maps species code to water column
type and to the fish, shellfish or other split; `Link_GSA_Marine regions_Mediterranean Sea.xlsx`
maps GFCM sub-areas (`sa1` to `sa27`) to FAO Mediterranean divisions (`37.x.x`), which is required
for any Mediterranean map; `Overuse_Total.xlsx` holds the 2018 FSI scores joined after allocation.

# Findings that must not be rediscovered

**2024 cannot be valued.** No Member State has reported any expenditure variable for 2024 except 23
energy cost figures, and only 11 of 22 reported any income. The latest fully valuable year is 2023.
2024 landings can be shown in physical terms with a provisional caveat only.

**Subsidies are already included.** Operating subsidies are `totdirsub` inside
`variable_group == "Income"`, so filtering to Income plus Expenditure captures them. Subsidies on
investments (`subinvest`) sit under `Capital` and stay excluded. Domenico will correct Box 1 so it
no longer appears to include them.

**Sub-region codes carry whitespace.** About 21,000 of 57,000 landing rows write `sa 21` rather than
`sa21`. Whitespace must be stripped before any recode or join, or the entire Mediterranean fails to
match silently.

**Eight species codes are duplicated** in the classification workbook (CAS, RKQ, SCY, SHR, SJA, SRK,
SSB, YRS). Each pair is identical; `distinct(species_code)` is a safe delete (Domenico, Sep 2026).

**`sa11.1` and `sa11.2` are recoded to `sa11`.** They are Mediterranean GFCM areas. The 2023
shapefile tonne anchors for Mediterranean and ROW therefore shift by that tonnage (about 20 t in
2023). NEAO, Baltic and Black Sea still match `Tonnes_23`.

**Type vs Type_1.** The classification workbook has no `Type_1`. `Type` is the water-column
typology (`fish_type`: benthic, benthopelagic, demersal, pelagic). `species_group` (fish /
shellfish / other) comes from the binary flags. Those names match methods Tables 4 to 13.

**Three bugs in the original were fixed** and are documented in callouts on the processing page:
the price table double counted collapsed sub-regions; the division guard tested the wrong column;
and the two published unit conversions were computed on different bases.

**Validation anchor.** Before the sa11 recode, 2023 landings in tonnes were NEAO 1,925,814, Baltic
436,672, Mediterranean 308,722, Black Sea 9,961, ROW 713,131. The first four matched the
`Tonnes_23` attributes the previous team baked into `Macro_Division_European_Sea.shp` to the
kilogram. After recoding `sa11.1` and `sa11.2` into the Mediterranean, only NEAO, Baltic and Black
Sea still match those integers; Med and ROW move by the sa11 decimals. Any other refactor of
`harmonise_sub_reg()` or `assign_sea_region()` must still explain any further movement.

# Geometry

All geometry now lives in `data/gisdata/marine_regions.gpkg`, one GeoPackage with eight layers, all
WGS 84, all FAO fishing area geometry. The eight source shapefiles and their 53 sidecar files were
converted by `data/gisdata/shapefiles_to_gpkg.R` and then deleted. Every field of every layer was
verified value for value against the shapefiles before deletion. The script cannot be rerun and is
kept only as a record.

- `sea_regions_macro`, 4 features. The base map. `Sea_name` holds NEAO, MED, Black and Baltic and
  joins to our `Sea_reg` after a label recode.
- `sea_regions_division`, 58 features. The detailed map, at FAO division and subdivision level.
- `baltic_sea` 10, `black_sea` 3, `mediterranean_sea` 7, `neao` 38. Subsets of the 58.
- `neao_fao_subdivisions`, 55 features. The finer, undissolved FAO geometry.
- `mediterranean_legacy`, 7 features. Same geometry as `mediterranean_sea`; the only surviving copy
  of the published 2023 Mediterranean attributes.

The `_modified` shapefiles were chosen as authoritative because Baltic plus Black plus Mediterranean
plus modified NEAO reproduce the F_CODE set of the 58 feature layer exactly, and the unmodified NEAO
does not. That modification was a dissolve of finer FAO subdivisions, the same collapse that
`sub_reg_recodes` performs in the processing routine.

Join on `F_CODE` or `F_DIVISION` against `sub_reg_clean`, never against the raw `sub_reg`. The
Mediterranean needs the GSA lookup because the layers are FAO `37.x.x` while the accounts are
`saNN`; Mediterranean maps are therefore coarser than Mediterranean tables. ROW was 21 percent of
2023 tonnage and cannot appear on a European map, so maps need a reconciliation note.

Several layers carry result columns the previous team joined in by hand in QGIS, and a `path` field
still pointing at `E:/pisando/.../8_Maps/`. Those are legacy. Field names drift between layers
(`OversusTon`, `OveruseTon`, `OverusTonn`, `OverTonnes`, `Over_Ton`) because DBF truncated them to
ten characters. Do not extend that pattern; keep geometry static and join results at render time.

`NAME_FR` and `NAME_ES` contain 74 U+FFFD replacement characters. The accents were destroyed by some
export before the files reached us and are unrecoverable; the DBF held zero Latin-1 accented bytes.
Use `NAME_EN`, which is clean.

**QGIS trap.** QGIS writes layer metadata sidecars that also use the `.qmd` extension. Quarto picked
those up as documents and the whole site build failed. Moving to GeoPackage removed them, and the
`render:` exclusion in `_quarto.yml` stays as a guard. Anything added under `data/` must stay
excluded.

# Conventions

Nominal euro is produced by the allocation. A single step at the end converts resource rent to
constant prices with Eurostat HICP for EU-27. The base year is `base_year` in the processing
parameters chunk and is currently 2010 (JRC decision, September 2026). Changing it is a one-line
edit. Tables report nominal euro and 2010 euro.

The processing page builds the 2008-2023 panel in one pass. `year` is part of every grouping key
and every gap-filling join, so there is no cross-year contamination. `reference_year` is kept for
the live 2023 checks and the dashboard headline figures.

The published GeoPackage is map grain only: one polygon per sea region or FAO division, with one
column per year for landings, resource rent (nominal and constant euro) and price. Species, country
and fleet stay in the RDS and Excel files. Splitting into three files, one per metric, would
triplicate the geometry for no gain.

**SUT grains in the methods report.** Eq. 1 is supply by marine region (`Sea_reg`). Eq. 2 is use by
landing country: STECF `country_code` is the three-letter Member State where the catch is landed
(BEL, BGR, CYP, DEU, DNK, ESP, EST, FIN, FRA, GRC, HRV, IRL, ITA, LTU, LVA, MLT, NLD, POL, PRT,
ROU, SVN, SWE). Tables 4 to 7 nest `species_group` (fish / shellfish / other)
with `fish_type` (workbook `Type`: benthic, benthopelagic, demersal, pelagic; unmatched codes are
"Not identified"). Tables 8 and 9 are sea region by landing country. Tables 10 to 13 and annexes
1-15 (2018) and 16-35 (2023) are the nested layout inside one sea: supply rows are FAO/ICES
sub-regions (`sub_reg_clean`); use rows are landing countries. Column structure is Fish
(benthopelagic, demersal, pelagic), Shellfish (benthic, benthopelagic, demersal, pelagic), Others
(Not identified), Total. Empty cells were printed as dashes. Supply totals must equal use totals
at every grain; Baltic 2018 physical is 679,442.38 t on both sides.

**EEZ naming compromise (client decision, 2026-09-01).** STECF has no coastal-state EEZ field, and
no EEZ polygons will arrive. The methods report already labelled FAO statistical areas as
"Baltic EEZ" (and the same stub on the use table, whose rows are countries). We follow that:
`sub_reg_clean` is published as EEZ in the supply tables, with an explanatory note that these are
FAO/GFCM fishing areas used as the spatial unit of supply, not legal exclusive economic zones.
Do not invent EEZ polygons. Maps stay on FAO layers in `marine_regions.gpkg`. If true EEZ geometry
ever appears, join a compact year-column summary without exploding polygons by year.

**SUT Excel deliverable.** Report-style accounting tables are written in the `write-outputs`
chunk of `documentation/methods/marine-processing/index.qmd` (`openxlsx2`). Source is
`fish_provisioning` (2008-2023). Pivot `species_group` × `fish_type` wide with `values_fill = 0`
and `names_expand = TRUE`. Three metrics: physical (tonnes), nominal thousand euro, thousand 2010
euro. For each metric, supply and use. Supply rows: `sub_reg_clean`, labelled EEZ, within
`Sea_reg`. Use rows: landing country (ISO names from `data/stecf/country_codes.csv`). Identity:
supply Total equals use Total at every grain. Europe Tables 2-9 sit at the top of each year
sheet; nested Tables 10-13 follow per sea. Keep the flat `fish_provisioning.xlsx` panel.

**Excel formatting.** Written in `write-outputs` with `openxlsx2`. Each stacked table has rows 1
to 3 JRC / title / year, row 4 blank, two-row bold headers (species group then fish type), width
10, 1 pt under headers, 1 pt above and below Total, `#,##0.00`. `writexl` is only for the flat
panel. Keep the writer in the processing page; no Python and no standalone helper file.

Replicable from our panel, one-to-one with `reference/marine-methods.docx` chapter 4:

- Tables 2-3: Europe SUT by fishing region (Baltic, Black, Mediterranean, NEAO, ROW, unknown).
  Use sits in the primary-sector / fisheries column, as in the report.
- Tables 4-5: Europe supply, nested fish type × fishing region, physical and monetary.
- Tables 6-7: Europe use, landing country × nested fish type, physical and monetary.
- Tables 8-9: landing country × fishing region (origin of the flow).
- Tables 10-13: Baltic nested SUT; supply by `sub_reg_clean` labelled EEZ; use by country.
- Annexes 1-15 and 16-35: the same nested SUT for Black Sea, Mediterranean, NEAO (and 2023
  vintages). We can also produce every year 2008-2023, not only 2018 and 2023.
- Tables 14-15: 2018 vs 2023 comparison (physical, and monetary at constant prices).

Out of scope for this SUT workbook: overuse as a column of the official SUT (it is a companion
overlay; see overuse below); blue carbon Tables 25-32.

**STECF has no EEZ field.** The three 2025 data-call workbooks were checked: fleet segment
(`STECF_25 07_EU Fleet Economic and Transversal data_fleet segment level.xlsx`), FAO landings
(`STECF_25 07_Landings_fao.xlsx`), and national level
(`STECF_25-07_EU Fleet Economic and Transversal data_national level.xlsx`). Geography in all three
is Member State of the fleet or landing (`country_code`, `country_name`) plus FAO/GFCM fishing area
of the catch (`sub_reg`, `supra_reg`). `geo_indicator` is outermost-region fleet geography (IC,
GP, MQ, RE, YT, GF, MA, Azores P2/P3, and so on). `codes__regions_sub-regions` maps sub-region to
FAO area and GSA. National `MS_landings` has species but no sub-region. That is why the tables
use `sub_reg_clean` under the EEZ label, with a note.

# Overuse / sustainability score

Do not fold this into Eq. 3 to 7. Apply it after allocation, as a last step like HICP deflation.
It is a companion overlay, not a column of the official SUT.

Source method: `reference/marine-methods.docx` chapter 4.5, and `reference/AP_Deliverable4.pdf`
(BioMESES Deliverable 4, Peris Tamayo 2024). Wild-capture **FSI** uses FAO GFCM (Mediterranean and
Black Sea) plus ICES (Baltic and NEAO); fishing-mortality score plus stock-abundance score; total
score maps to risk (methods Table 17 / D4 Table 1): critical -4.00 to -0.01, zero or no data 0,
high 0.01 to 3.44, intermediate 3.45 to 5.44, low 5.45 to 7.44, riskless 7.45 to 8.00. Methods
Table 18 converts the integer score to an overuse share of actual flow: critical 100%, score 1 ->
90%, 2 -> 70%, 3 -> 50%, 4 -> 40%, 5 -> 30%, 6 -> 20%, 7 -> 10%, 8 -> 0%. Sustainable flow is (1 - share)
times landings; overuse is share times landings; monetary overuse is that times resource rent.
Restricted to the top species with stock assessments (methods Table 19; D4 is the top 10 from
Deliverable 3). Reported at Europe, marine region, and country EEZ. Aquaculture **ASI** is a
separate eutrophication score from EMODnet points; out of scope unless asked.

`data/lookup/Overuse_Total.xlsx` is a 2018-era worked example. The processing page joins the
integer `Score` on `species_code` plus sea and reapplies Table 18; it does not reuse the
pre-multiplied Overuse columns. Scores differ by sea (COD is 1 in the Baltic and 2 in NEAO).
Species coverage differs by sea (TUR only in the Black Sea). Scores are held constant across
2008-2023 until a new stock-assessment vintage arrives. D4 annex zips A1-A10 are not in this repo.

